From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:11:44 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: initialise GBM for an external EGLDisplay only on request

virgl_gbm_init(-1) opens the first usable /dev/dri/renderD* and makes it the
global gbm device. Whether that helps or hurts depends on the guest, so it
cannot be decided at build time:

 - An AAOS guest compositing with SkiaVk needs it. Without a gbm device
   vrend_pipe_resource_export_fd() returns VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_INVALID, the
   render-server proxy aborts the attach silently, and the venus context never
   learns the resource -- vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA fails with
   "invalid res_id" and the compositor dies a few seconds after reporting that
   its Vulkan renderer came up.
 - An AGL/weston guest on the same host does not, and the extra device costs a
   second open of the render node per device model.

So gate it on VIRGL_EXTERNAL_GBM in the environment, which the device model
sets per guest through the libxl qemu_envs list. Default off keeps the
behaviour of an unpatched build for anyone who does not set it, and the test is
"set and not 0" rather than "set", so VIRGL_EXTERNAL_GBM=0 disables it as a
reader would expect.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [the switch is a private environment variable;
 upstream would want it expressed in the renderer API or the context flags]
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c
@@ -605,8 +605,48 @@ struct virgl_egl *virgl_egl_init_external(EGLDisplay egl_display)
       goto fail;
 
 #ifdef ENABLE_GBM
-   gbm = virgl_gbm_init(-1);
-   egl->gbm = gbm;
+   /* Initialise the global gbm only when the device model asks for it.
+    *
+    * This assignment is load-bearing in two opposite directions, and which one matters
+    * depends on the guest, so it cannot be decided at build time:
+    *
+    *  * A non-NULL global gbm removes the early return in vrend_resource_gbm_init()
+    *    (vrend_renderer.c), so every VIRGL_BIND_SHARED guest resource is backed by a GBM
+    *    buffer object. Only such a resource can be exported as a dma-buf by
+    *    vrend_pipe_resource_export_fd(), and that export is what lets a venus context
+    *    import a guest gralloc buffer. An Android guest compositing with Vulkan needs it:
+    *    without it every vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA fails with a CS error and the
+    *    guest's Venus aborts in vn_relax waiting for the reply.
+    *
+    *  * On V3D, GBM allocation is forced linear, which loses UIF tiling. Measured on this
+    *    board with the AGL instrument cluster on GL: 60.0 fps / 0.32 host cores / 81.2 %
+    *    GPU with gbm NULL, against 29.0 / 0.52 / 42.9 % with gbm initialised. A GL guest
+    *    pays half its frame rate for a feature it never uses.
+    *
+    * The device models are separate processes, so an environment variable lets each guest
+    * choose: DomA's qemu_envs sets VIRGL_EXTERNAL_GBM=1 and gets Vulkan compositing, DomU
+    * leaves it unset and keeps 60 fps. Default off = the behaviour a GL guest wants.
+    *
+    * The permanent fix is upstream -- virglrenderer MR 1654 allocates GBM buffers with
+    * a tiled modifier chosen through Vulkan, which removes the linear penalty and with
+    * it the reason for this switch -- but it is NOT carried here (MR 1654, 652e8d8b,
+    * is in no release tag, and it needs a paired guest change to be correct; see the
+    * recipe's tiled-GBM section). Until then the guest that pays the penalty, DomU, is
+    * deliberately run with gbm unset: that is the configuration whose 60 fps was
+    * measured.
+    *
+    * "Set and not 0", not merely "set": the obvious getenv() != NULL test would make
+    * VIRGL_EXTERNAL_GBM=0 turn the feature ON, which is the opposite of what anyone
+    * writing that would mean.
+    */
+   {
+      const char *want_gbm = getenv("VIRGL_EXTERNAL_GBM");
+
+      if (want_gbm && want_gbm[0] && strcmp(want_gbm, "0")) {
+         gbm = virgl_gbm_init(-1);
+         egl->gbm = gbm;
+      }
+   }
 #endif
 
    virgl_egl_win32_init(egl);
